00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.270 align:start position:0%
 
good morning  it's my great pleasure to be  participating in this celebration of the  30th anniversary of the office of  research on women's health  and i'm going to be talking about  nccih's focus on the health of women  we know that complementary and  integrative health approaches are very  uh used very uh extensively by women 30  percent 37 percent of women compared  with 29 of men  use some a form of complementary  health  and so what are these types of  approaches  we describe them as being in sort of  three broad categories of dietary  psychological and physical  therapies and practices that include  things like for example  nutritional supplements probiotics or  meditation mindfulness-based stress  reduction or a physical therapy such as  manual therapies and of course there's  overlap between these categories for  example yoga is a combination of  psychological and physical component  and we know that uh if we ask  why what is the reason why people use  these types of approaches  a lot of people say that they're using  it for general health wellness  well-being  but a certain percentages of the  population use them for specific health  conditions and what are these health  conditions um when we ask specifically  you can see that overwhelmingly  pain is is a  back pain for example neck pain joint  pain and other musculoskeletal pain are  the four  specific health conditions that are most  frequently cited by by individuals for  their use of these complementary  interventions  and we know that women tend to have  disproportionately greater amounts of  both pain and high impact for chronic  pain  so this is important uh for for the  health of women that we understand how  best to uh to  encourage and the rational use of these  therapies that we do proper research to  understand are these therapies  actually useful are they effective are  they safe  and when we look for our portfolio on  research that relates to women's health  you can see that the pain is very well  represented as well as other topics such  as uh general health well-being stress  etc  so uh talking about pain first um we are  very fortunate that we have been able to  um contribute uh to participate in a  very important heal initiative which  stands for health to end addiction long  term  this is a trans nih effort that is you  that is aimed at  addressing both the opioid epidemic and  also the pain epidemic because we know  that there is a lot of uh  a very extensive uh problem with pain in  this country that  unfortunately all too often results in  the  prescription of opioids and so um nccih  has participated in a uh has been  leading actually a  program called prism which is uh  stands for pragmatic and implementation  studies for the management of pain to  reduce opioid prescribing these are  non-pharmacological approaches these are  examples here for example using uh  transcutaneous electrical nerve  stimulation mindfulness stress reduction  acupuncture and  telehealth for example uh in  in rural communities as well as uh  guided relaxation so um these are kind  of examples of studies that we've been  able to uh fund through the heal  initiative  another important area is uh natural  products and a lot of people use natural  products uh many of them of people in in  in the hope of of helping with pain it's  very important that we understand both  uh the potential use of these products  but also their safety and potential  interactions uh with uh medications for  example or other natural products that  people might be might be taking so for  that uh aim we we held a workshop back  in 2019  that was in collaboration uh with an  inds and ncats and uh this was a very uh  very good workshop in that it really led  to a program that is again in  collaboration with uh not only in ids  and ncaas but also nci um to basically  mine both electronic databases and also  uh natural product libraries um that  already exists that have extensive  collections of natural products that can  then be screened for in phenotypic  assays uh in high throughput screening  for effectiveness in uh targets that  could be um for analgesia and so this  has led to a very nice  collaborative program uh to um very  explore uh and discover  new potential analgesics that derive  from natural products  now be cannabinoids is all are also very  uh much at the front of people's minds  because they're used a lot people use  cannabinoids especially products such as  cbd uh that is available  over the counter and uh we wanted to  make sure that we understand uh if  how effective these are and how safe  these are and so in 2019 uh we made 11  awards to explore pain relieving  properties and mechanisms of actions of  of the many different compounds that are  uh in cannabis including uh cannabinoids  minor terpenes  and uh so  this is again just gives you an idea of  the portfolio and and and and our our um  are the breadth of our research in pain  but as i mentioned before  pain is not the only reason right where  people like people use uh complementary  and integrative health approaches  general health and and well-being is  extremely important in some cases really  in some for example for yoga or um  it was overwhelmingly the reason why  people use this uh this inter this this  these practices or meditation for  example it's for promoting uh health and  well-being and so  we need to understand first of all how  do you do research  on well-being um we are very much geared  towards understanding uh disease right  and we have a lot of sophisticated  measurements of symptoms and and and  uh and disease biomarkers for example  what are the biomarkers of of well-being  and health we need to understand that  right so we had a roundtable in 2018 on  emotional well-being um and uh this uh  was uh uh resulted uh again in a a a  collaboration uh in in the  we funded five research networks um that  will be launched in december 2020  looking at uh ontology of emotional  well-being how do you characterize it  how do you measure it mechanisms  biomarkers uh prevention and also  technology and outcomes measure  development so this is again an effort  that is very sort of  cross-disciplinary and that will help in  further the research on this very  important topic  another exciting initiative that we have  again another uh collaboration this time  between nih the kennedy center and the  national endowments for the art arts um  is the sound health initiative um this  is the goal of this initiative which was  spearheaded by dr francis collins  about is to really increase our  understanding of how music affects  health uh with uh emphasis on basic  neuroscience and also potential uh  clinical applications and just like like  as many of these other initiatives we  really started with a workshop uh that  really brought together an amazing uh  collaboration this was uh back in 2017  uh between again the kennedy center the  nea and many  music therapy professional and advocacy  groups um there was a uh  dr collins participated here with renee  fleming who's a well-known uh  soprano who is very active in this area  of promoting the understanding of music  and uh and its use in in  promoting health  and uh this panel uh sound health  research resulted in  a an initiative that we are now uh  we have funded three grants now in this  area evaluating for uh  example evaluating the impact of singing  our interventions on the markers of  cardiovascular health in older patients  uh the effect of music-based  intervention on neurodevelopment and  pain response and pre-term infants and  also using uh self-generated rhythmic  cues to enhance gait in parkinson's  disease so this is just the beginning  we're really excited about  how we may better understand uh how uh  how music and other arts but for example  uh visual arts  can be um  can be used for promoting health  another area that we're very much  interested in is the connections between  the the mind and the body um one of the  areas where we have developed this is in  the concept of inner reception um this  is the a very important  area of science that is really in need  of further development and so we started  it with a a workshop uh in collaboration  when you see all of these institutes uh  this was sponsored by the  uh  neuroscience blueprint initiative and  what it did so what is inter-reception  it's really the processes by which our  body senses interprets integrates and  regulates signals from within itself so  how does your brain for example perceive  when you are hungry or thirsty or tired  um what are the what are the signals  that come from our body that the brain  then has to the nervous system has to  integrate this is this is really a very  fascinating area that has a lot of uh  bearing understanding this has a lot of  bearing in our understanding of for  example meditation or yoga where you  really focus on your breathing how is it  that you sense that and what information  does it give you  and what can you do about it  so um this workshop uh  is uh was was uh uh in 2019 and explored  various aspects of the neural circuitry  underlying these dynamic interception  interactions the interreceptive  processes associated with certain  diseases and disorders modulating these  processes potentially with the different  interventions and therapies and also  developing the methodology and  technology to better uh do this research  and so we have an rfa that is out the  applications are due soon december 18th  and the goal is to enhance our  fundamental understanding of  interreception with a specific focus on  determining the function of these neural  circuits  so i've talked to uh just this is just a  snapshot summary of that i gave you of  some of the you know exciting  initiatives that we've been involved  involved in but i i'm just want to  finish with this slide that shows you  how this fits into the bigger picture  um nccih is we are in the middle of  developing a new strategic plan and what  we're focusing on is whole person health  research how does all this fit well uh  we we know that um whole the whole  person in health we think about this as  a two axis so on the vertical axis axis  here we have the integration of the sort  of biocycle social elements  as well as the environment because the  environment is very important of course  we know our physical environment as well  as our social environment it's very  important in in in determining uh our  health and within the physiological  domains you have all of these different  systems neural respiratory  cardiovascular digestive etc that need  to interact in that the interception  example i gave you is just an example of  that  and then on the horizontal axis you have  what we call the uh bi-directional  health disease continuum know that  people are traveling along this  continuum back and forth it's not just  one way towards disease and death you  can go back towards health and we think  that a lot of these pharmacological and  non-pharmacological interventions can be  very helpful in helping people  to uh to go back in and restore their  health after they've been sick  or if they have a chronic illness to  help them gain  regain  health and improve and this happens on  the different planes on the  physiological plane as well as in the  social and psychological plane and  symptom management of course is part of  this at every step so managing pain  is part of this of this continuum  so i hope that this kind of gives you an  idea of the exciting uh some of the  exciting initiatives that we're  developing and of course all of this is  relevant to the health of women um and  we really look forward to uh  further discussions with uh orweh on on  how we can further collaborate on on  many of these initiatives  thank you

